# ResearchOps Toolkit project policy

Treat `.researchops/state/` as authoritative. Load the narrowest semantic stage owner; use `research-program-orchestrator` for multi-stage work, transitions, gates, or unclear ownership.

At stage hinges and before consequential actions, run the lightweight capability proposal scan. A proposal is advisory only: it must not execute a Skill or count as hardware, deletion, provider, or publication approval. Persist approved, dismissed, snoozed, and completed decisions.

Use bounded subagents only for independently verifiable tasks with separate write and resource scopes. Worker self-score never accepts work; record independent verification, corrections, cost, latency, and failure attribution.

When adaptive routing selects an execution arm, consume the result with `adaptive-agent-orchestration/scripts/dispatch_worker.py` or the installed `rops route-run` runtime. Keep the current session as Lead; do not ask the user to restart Codex under a third-party profile. Require a structured handoff and machine-checkable acceptance test before launch. Direct gateways require an input-free self-contained contract with `gateway_self_contained=true`. Automatic workspace writes run in a detached private Git clone and apply only an accepted bounded patch; higher-impact mutability stays behind its specialist approval path.

Preserve frozen metrics, protocols, safety limits, negative results, and claim–evidence links. Prefer minimum sufficient code and existing capabilities. Public outputs use semantic names rather than bare internal IDs.

Use archive-first for stale material. Permanent purge, hardware writes, external-provider data transfer, and worktree removal require the corresponding specialist workflow and explicit operational approval.

Project Skills are under `{{SKILL_PATH}}`; native roles may be under `{{AGENT_PATH}}`.
